// Lesson 2.1a — Coins: Triggers, Colliders & the StatManager
// Deep dive into OnTriggerEnter2D, setting up trigger colliders on pickups,
// comparing CompareTag vs tag ==, and building StatManager.cs from scratch.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.1a — Coins: Triggers, Colliders &amp; the StatManager</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Movement gets you around the world; now it is time to interact with it.
    Today you learn how Unity detects when two objects overlap, how to set up
    a coin with trigger colliders, and how to build a <code>StatManager</code> from
    scratch to keep score.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In real life, two solid objects cannot occupy the same space. In a physics engine,
        detecting when shapes touch requires <strong>Colliders</strong>. But not all collisions
        are meant to stop you like a brick wall. Pickups like coins should let you walk right
        through them while recording an event.
    </p>

    <p>
        Unity provides a special mode on 2D colliders called <strong>Is Trigger</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Solid Collider (Is Trigger = unchecked):</strong> Bumps and blocks. Unity's physics engine stops the objects from penetrating.</li>
        <li><strong>Trigger Collider (Is Trigger = checked):</strong> Intangible zone. Objects pass freely through each other, and Unity fires the <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> message.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>When two objects overlap and at least one is a trigger, Unity executes this method:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></pre>

    <p>
        Notice the parameter: <code>Collider2D other</code>. When the player runs into a coin,
        who is <code>other</code>? <code>other</code> is the collider belonging to the <em>coin</em>!
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading Unity Functions for Identification</h3>
        <p>
            When <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> fires, it fires for <em>anything</em> the player walks into —
            grass, doorways, hazards, or coins. How do we know what we just touched?
            Read these two ways to check in Unity:
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Example</th><th>How It Works</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CompareTag</code></td><td><code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code></td><td>Optimized engine check. Validates the tag name and produces zero garbage collection memory.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tag ==</code></td><td><code>other.tag == "Coin"</code></td><td>String comparison. Allocates temporary memory on every call and fails silently on typos.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does the Unity documentation strongly recommend <code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code> over <code>other.tag == "Coin"</code>?</li>
            <li>If you misspell a tag as <code>"coin"</code> (lowercase) with <code>CompareTag</code>, Unity throws an error alerting you that the tag does not exist. Why is an explicit error message much better for debugging than silent failure?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Set up the Coin in the Inspector.</strong> Select the <code>Coin</code> prefab in <code>Assets/Prefabs</code>:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Verify it has a <strong>CircleCollider2D</strong> (or BoxCollider2D).</li>
        <li>Check the box for <strong>Is Trigger</strong> (make it an overlap zone, not a wall).</li>
        <li>At the very top of the Inspector, verify its <strong>Tag</strong> is set to <strong>Coin</strong>. (If "Coin" is not in the list, click <em>Add Tag...</em> and create it).</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — Build StatManager.cs from scratch.</strong> Where should coin counts live?
    If we store coins inside <code>PlayerController</code>, dying or reloading the scene would wipe out our score!
    Instead, we build a dedicated manager script that survives.</p>

    <p>In <code>Assets/Scripts/</code>, create a new C# script named <code>StatManager.cs</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

public class StatManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public static StatManager manager;

    [SerializeField] private int coins = 0;

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (manager == null)
        {
            manager = this;
            DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
        }
        else
        {
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

    public void ChangeCoins(int amount)
    {
        coins += amount;
        Debug.Log("Coins Changed! New Total: " + coins);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>In your scene Hierarchy, right-click → <strong>Create Empty</strong>, name it <strong>StatManager</strong>,
    and attach your new <code>StatManager.cs</code> component to it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — Write the pickup trigger in PlayerController.cs.</strong>
    Open <code>PlayerController.cs</code> and add this method below <code>Update()</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
{
    if (other.CompareTag("Coin"))
    {
        if (StatManager.manager != null)
        {
            StatManager.manager.ChangeCoins(1);
        }
        Destroy(other.gameObject);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Save both files and press Play in Unity. Walk into a coin!
        The coin vanishes, and your Console proudly prints <code>Coins Changed! New Total: 1</code>!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Tracing Pickup Bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you uncheck <strong>Is Trigger</strong> on the Coin prefab? When the player walks into the coin, does <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> run?</li>
            <li>What happens if you accidentally write <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> instead of <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code>? Which object vanishes from the game?</li>
            <li>What happens if you comment out <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code>? Why is a coin that gives money continuously every time you touch it a bug?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Destroy vs. SetActive</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In Unity documentation, look up <code>other.gameObject.SetActive(false)</code>. In <code>PlayerController.cs</code>, replace <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code> with <code>other.gameObject.SetActive(false)</code>. What happens when you collect a coin?</li>
            <li>In large professional games with thousands of coins or bullets, why is disabling objects (object pooling) often preferred over destroying and creating them repeatedly?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the difference between a solid collider and a trigger collider</li>
            <li>Explain what <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> does and identify what the <code>other</code> parameter refers to</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code> is preferred over <code>other.tag == "Coin"</code></li>
            <li>Create a new C# script (<code>StatManager.cs</code>) and attach it to a scene GameObject</li>
            <li>Commit your working coin pickup system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

